1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,897
6 km under the sea, the water will squeeze this titanium ball — with almost 1,00,000 tonnes.

2
00:00:07,239 --> 00:00:10,016
Imagine you're inside it, with two other Indians.

3
00:00:10,490 --> 00:00:14,579
This ball is the heart of India's first deep-sea sub for people.

4
00:00:14,897 --> 00:00:16,510
Its name is Matsya.

5
00:00:16,944 --> 00:00:21,735
Your whole world is five pieces of titanium, joined by seven welds.

6
00:00:22,105 --> 00:00:23,457
Now here's the strange part.

7
00:00:23,797 --> 00:00:25,650
ISRO is known for rockets.

8
00:00:25,758 --> 00:00:28,626
But ISRO's hardest weld wasn't for a rocket.

9
00:00:28,768 --> 00:00:30,009
It was for this ball.

10
00:00:30,204 --> 00:00:34,724
That weld was 4 times thicker than their welding machine could handle before.

11
00:00:35,013 --> 00:00:37,168
It took about 700 tries.

12
00:00:37,489 --> 00:00:38,921
So why was it so hard?

13
00:00:39,167 --> 00:00:41,838
And after Titan, how will they know it holds?

14
00:00:42,226 --> 00:00:43,937
By the end, you'll know both.

15
00:00:44,255 --> 00:00:48,702
First, I want you to feel what 6 km of sea actually does.

16
00:00:49,022 --> 00:00:54,735
Every 10 metres you go down, the sea presses on you as hard as all the air above you.

17
00:00:55,072 --> 00:00:55,747
Once more.

18
00:00:56,089 --> 00:00:56,844
And again.

19
00:00:57,177 --> 00:00:58,290
And again.

20
00:00:58,632 --> 00:01:01,532
At 6,000 metres, hold out your finger.

21
00:01:01,837 --> 00:01:05,666
The water would press on your fingernail with about 600 kilos.

22
00:01:05,913 --> 00:01:09,279
That's a full-grown buffalo, standing on one fingernail.

23
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:11,986
Now cover the whole ball with fingernails.

24
00:01:12,203 --> 00:01:14,687
Every one of them carries a buffalo.

25
00:01:15,019 --> 00:01:21,907
Add it all up, and the sea squeezes this ball with almost 1,00,000 tonnes, from every side at once.

26
00:01:22,263 --> 00:01:25,121
So you're probably asking the same question I asked.

27
00:01:25,328 --> 00:01:26,913
Could this end like Titan?

28
00:01:27,262 --> 00:01:28,311
You may remember it.

29
00:01:28,511 --> 00:01:33,180
In June 2023, Titan broke apart on its way down to the Titanic.

30
00:01:33,338 --> 00:01:34,579
Five people died.

31
00:01:35,504 --> 00:01:38,494
Its hull, as you may know, was carbon fibre.

32
00:01:38,704 --> 00:01:44,098
After earlier dives, its layers had started to come apart, and it kept diving anyway.

33
00:01:44,288 --> 00:01:48,025
The US Coast Guard said the company's own failures caused it.

34
00:01:48,189 --> 00:01:49,818
So what's different here?

35
00:01:50,101 --> 00:01:50,943
Three things.

36
00:01:51,091 --> 00:01:56,368
The material is titanium, the metal deep-sea subs have trusted for more than 50 years.

37
00:01:56,524 --> 00:01:58,107
The shape is a ball.

38
00:01:58,275 --> 00:02:02,119
And an outside inspection company checks and approves the work.

39
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,823
For Matsya, that company is from Norway.

40
00:02:04,946 --> 00:02:07,078
And if something goes wrong down there?

41
00:02:07,231 --> 00:02:10,760
The crew can drop heavy weights, and the sub rises back up.

42
00:02:10,865 --> 00:02:13,854
And they carry enough air for 96 hours.

43
00:02:14,051 --> 00:02:15,473
But why a ball?

44
00:02:15,542 --> 00:02:18,191
Why not a long tube, like a normal submarine?

45
00:02:18,325 --> 00:02:19,858
Squeeze a ball in your hand.

46
00:02:19,946 --> 00:02:22,736
You can feel the push spread all the way round.

47
00:02:22,864 --> 00:02:26,317
There's no corner and no flat side where the pressure can win.

48
00:02:26,389 --> 00:02:30,070
Even America's famous deep-sea sub, Alvin, uses a ball.

49
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:33,209
Its first one, in 1964, was steel.

50
00:02:33,283 --> 00:02:39,089
In 1973, they changed it to titanium, and Alvin could go almost twice as deep.

51
00:02:39,185 --> 00:02:40,872
India walked the same road.

52
00:02:40,947 --> 00:02:46,931
In 2021, it lowered a test ball of plain steel 600 metres into the Bay of Bengal.

53
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:48,522
Then came titanium.

54
00:02:48,642 --> 00:02:51,245
And this, you see, is where ISRO comes in.

55
00:02:51,333 --> 00:02:53,249
Remember that weld I told you about?

56
00:02:53,329 --> 00:02:56,240
You can't make a ball this big in one piece.

57
00:02:56,290 --> 00:02:57,670
So how do you make it?

58
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:00,130
ISRO's drawing shows five pieces.

59
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:01,160
A top hatch.

60
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:02,130
Three rings.

61
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:03,250
A bottom dome.

62
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:06,240
Together, they weigh about 5.5 tonnes.

63
00:03:06,843 --> 00:03:08,490
Seven welds join them.

64
00:03:08,676 --> 00:03:11,356
Four go right round the ball, like belts.

65
00:03:11,621 --> 00:03:13,413
Three go round its windows.

66
00:03:13,662 --> 00:03:15,273
Here's the problem you'd face.

67
00:03:15,466 --> 00:03:17,229
Titanium is fussy.

68
00:03:17,535 --> 00:03:21,235
Heat it in normal air, and it grabs oxygen and nitrogen.

69
00:03:21,464 --> 00:03:26,143
The weld fills with tiny holes, and it becomes weak and easy to crack.

70
00:03:26,288 --> 00:03:28,831
So you can't weld it with a normal flame.

71
00:03:29,111 --> 00:03:29,832
What do you do?

72
00:03:30,129 --> 00:03:34,231
ISRO used a beam of electrons, fired inside a vacuum.

73
00:03:34,427 --> 00:03:36,731
No air, so nothing can get in.

74
00:03:37,037 --> 00:03:38,540
But there was a catch.

75
00:03:38,672 --> 00:03:43,024
ISRO's welding centre in Bengaluru could weld up to 2 cm thick.

76
00:03:43,231 --> 00:03:44,933
This wall is 8.

77
00:03:45,124 --> 00:03:47,934
So they made their machine much more powerful.

78
00:03:48,139 --> 00:03:49,564
Then they practised.

79
00:03:49,684 --> 00:03:53,996
About 700 trial welds, to get the settings exactly right.

80
00:03:54,234 --> 00:03:55,955
Then came the real thing.

81
00:03:56,144 --> 00:03:59,027
One of the big welds that go right round the ball.

82
00:03:59,211 --> 00:04:02,516
8 cm deep, and about 7 metres long.

83
00:04:02,699 --> 00:04:05,520
It took 32 minutes, in one go.

84
00:04:05,735 --> 00:04:10,353
ISRO says it was the first time anyone in India had made a weld like this.

85
00:04:10,605 --> 00:04:12,895
But then, how do you know it's good inside?

86
00:04:13,058 --> 00:04:14,788
You can't cut it open, can you?

87
00:04:14,962 --> 00:04:20,513
So they built X-ray machines strong enough to see through 8 cm of titanium.

88
00:04:20,635 --> 00:04:23,890
They used sound waves too, listening for hidden cracks.

89
00:04:24,018 --> 00:04:26,993
And an outside agency has to certify it.

90
00:04:27,214 --> 00:04:28,849
Now step inside with me.

91
00:04:29,085 --> 00:04:34,207
The wall is 8 cm thick, so inside, you get just over 2 metres.

92
00:04:34,309 --> 00:04:38,696
Lie down in there, and your head and your feet would almost touch the walls.

93
00:04:39,726 --> 00:04:43,880
You and two others sit here, for a normal dive of 12 hours.

94
00:04:44,164 --> 00:04:45,475
Oxygen comes in.

95
00:04:45,501 --> 00:04:48,464
The carbon dioxide you breathe out gets cleaned away.

96
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:50,863
And who will sit where you're sitting?

97
00:04:51,110 --> 00:04:57,552
In August 2025, India sent its first two trained people down in a French sub called Nautile.

98
00:04:57,715 --> 00:05:01,749
Raju Ramesh, a scientist, reached about 4,000 metres.

99
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:07,703
The next day, Commander Jatinder Pal Singh, a retired Navy officer, reached about 5,000.

100
00:05:07,829 --> 00:05:10,238
So is Matsya ready, do you think?

101
00:05:10,484 --> 00:05:11,334
Not quite.

102
00:05:11,383 --> 00:05:12,940
There's one more problem.

103
00:05:13,107 --> 00:05:15,424
This ball is only part of the sub.

104
00:05:15,542 --> 00:05:19,316
Around it go batteries, lights, motors and arms.

105
00:05:19,404 --> 00:05:22,951
Put it all together, and the whole thing sinks like a stone.

106
00:05:23,041 --> 00:05:25,493
So it's wrapped in a special foam.

107
00:05:25,573 --> 00:05:30,242
The foam is full of tiny hollow glass bubbles, set in hard plastic.

108
00:05:30,307 --> 00:05:32,227
The titanium takes the squeeze.

109
00:05:32,285 --> 00:05:34,380
The foam brings everyone home.

110
00:05:34,498 --> 00:05:38,261
That foam had to come from France, and it arrived late.

111
00:05:38,338 --> 00:05:42,986
Then, early this year, officials said a small crack was found in its cover.

112
00:05:43,056 --> 00:05:46,549
That held up the first test dive, to 500 metres.

113
00:05:46,590 --> 00:05:52,134
On 6 November, Matsya finished another round of trials near Chennai, without people inside.

114
00:05:52,175 --> 00:05:57,141
Officials have said the full 6 km dive could come in 2027.

115
00:05:57,200 --> 00:05:59,317
So why do all this, you ask?

116
00:05:59,435 --> 00:06:01,264
What's down there that's worth it?

117
00:06:02,664 --> 00:06:06,359
6,000 metres down, you'd see no sunlight at all.

118
00:06:06,602 --> 00:06:09,930
Your lights are the only lights for 6 km.

119
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:13,707
Outside your window, the water is close to freezing.

120
00:06:13,873 --> 00:06:18,139
And the seafloor is covered with black lumps of metal, like stones.

121
00:06:18,437 --> 00:06:23,790
India has the right to explore a big patch of that seabed, in the middle of the Indian Ocean.

122
00:06:24,039 --> 00:06:26,656
It has held that right since 2002.

123
00:06:26,850 --> 00:06:32,182
In 2022, an Indian robot sub surveyed it, more than 5 km down.

124
00:06:32,323 --> 00:06:35,425
Those lumps hold nickel, copper and cobalt.

125
00:06:35,543 --> 00:06:38,553
Metals that go into batteries, like the one in your phone.

126
00:06:38,662 --> 00:06:40,400
But let me be honest with you.

127
00:06:40,533 --> 00:06:42,826
Nobody is allowed to mine there yet.

128
00:06:42,992 --> 00:06:45,123
The world is still writing the rules.

129
00:06:45,242 --> 00:06:47,364
Matsya is not a mining machine.

130
00:06:47,504 --> 00:06:48,807
It's a pair of eyes.

131
00:06:48,914 --> 00:06:51,365
And you might ask, what about China?

132
00:06:51,522 --> 00:06:57,533
Its sub has already taken people to the deepest point on Earth, almost 11,000 metres.

133
00:06:57,636 --> 00:07:02,738
India's first goal is 6,000, deep enough for the seabed it wants to explore.

134
00:07:02,834 --> 00:07:06,200
Now, one last time, come back inside the ball with me.

135
00:07:06,356 --> 00:07:07,494
I'll be straight with you.

136
00:07:07,608 --> 00:07:10,733
Matsya has not been to sea with people in it yet.

137
00:07:10,822 --> 00:07:16,066
And no public report says this titanium ball has been tested at full pressure yet.

138
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:18,961
So the last test will be the sea itself.

139
00:07:19,079 --> 00:07:19,935
One dive.

140
00:07:20,103 --> 00:07:21,396
6 km.

141
00:07:21,458 --> 00:07:24,320
Every number in this film, checked at once.

142
00:07:24,417 --> 00:07:26,020
This story isn't over.

143
00:07:26,127 --> 00:07:29,299
When Matsya makes its first deep dive, we'll be there.

144
00:07:29,376 --> 00:07:32,032
Subscribe to Be Practical, so you're there too.
